{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>וארעה</b> (and I shall pasture): The meaning is that HaShem pastures them now, before the time of the rise of the Hasmoneans. Now he recounts by way of parable. It is also correct that he called them צאן ההרגה (flock of slaughter), for so they were in their exile to Babylonia; therefore he recounts the evils that passed over them, and now upon their return to Jerusalem he pastures them." "And the meaning of לכן עניי הצאן (therefore the poor of the flock): Because there are poor among the flock, I needed to take two staffs, and these are Zerubbabel, governor of Judah, and Nehemiah the Tirshatha. And it is written of Nehemiah: 'After the death of Zerubbavel he too was commanded to be governor in the land of Judah.' And he called one No'am, from the root of נעים (pleasant), and Hovalim from the root of תחבולות (schemes), and they were called staffs and not shepherds, because they were not kings." "<b>והנה ארעה את הצאן</b> (and so I pastured the flock): at first, for Israel came with Zerubbavel." "And the meaning of <b>וארעה את הצאן</b> (and I pastured the flock): until a known time when three shepherds died."], :book :zechariah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "The reading 'he called one No'am' (ויקראו נועם) appears to refer to the naming of the two staffs in v. 7 (one called נעם, the other חובלים); translated hyper-literally as the syntax stands."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Zecharia -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/zecharya"] ["1" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=25&perek=5&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:46:46.729313981Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}